<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03Z1">Warning <span data-tag="03Z1">8.5.1.24</span>.</a> If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is (the nerve of) an ordinary category, then a retraction diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be identified with a pair of morphisms $i: Y \rightarrow X$ and $r: X \rightarrow Y$ satisfying the condition $r \circ i = \operatorname{id}_{Y}$. Beware that, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a general $\infty $-category, then a retraction diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  X \ar [dr]^{ r } &amp;  \\ Y \ar [ur]^{i} \ar [rr]^{ \operatorname{id}_ Y } &amp;  &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p> generally cannot be recovered (even up to isomorphism) from the morphisms $i$ and $r$ alone: one also needs a homotopy which witnesses the identity $[r] \circ [i] = [ \operatorname{id}_{Y} ]$ in the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. </p>
</article>
